From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 16:09:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] d3d9, d3d11: Share textures within the process without
 external memory

Without VK_KHR_external_memory_win32 (Wine on the PS5) a texture cannot
be shared through a memory handle. A game whose Direct3D 9Ex device
decodes its movies into a shared texture that its Direct3D 11 device
opens (Koei Tecmo's movie player: Samurai Warriors 4 DX, Warriors
Orochi 3) then never shows them: D3D9 fails to export the texture and
D3D11's OpenSharedResource finds no handle.

Both devices are in the same process, so they can share the image
itself once they share a Vulkan device:

- d3d11.dll keeps a list of its devices' DxvkDevices and of textures
  shared within the process, and exports the calls that use it
  (DXVKLocalShare*), which d3d9.dll finds with GetProcAddress.
- Where external memory is missing, a D3D9Ex device made while a D3D11
  device lives runs on that device's DxvkDevice, when it has the
  features D3D9 needs (d3d9.shareD3D11Device = False turns this off).
- A D3D9 texture made shared there is listed instead of exported, kept
  in one layout as an exported one is, and gets the list's handle.
- D3D11's OpenSharedResource takes a listed image of its own device and
  makes a texture standing for it, checked against its description.
- Resource cookies count from a start of each DLL's own, since the
  device's allocator tells resources apart by cookie and both DLLs now
  make resources on one device.
- Both DLLs stay loaded once a device is shared, since each one's
  objects live on in the other's.
